## THE HONEST STANCE

The honest stance is not to claim certainty. The honest stance is not to worship logic. The honest stance is to remember:

> We built these tools.
> We named these patterns.
> We projected these ideas.
> We designed logic to help us live together.
> It is useful only when it serves that purpose.
> When logic separates us, it fails.
> When logic harms, it is misused.
> When logic forgets its purpose, it becomes dogma.
> We are responsible for how we use it.
> We are responsible to each other.
> That responsibility—ethics—comes first.

---

## WHY THIS MATTERS

### For Scientists
You are not discovering timeless truths. You are naming patterns that help us navigate. That is honorable. But the *value* of your work is not in its correspondence to "reality"—it is in its service to human flourishing. Ask not only "Is this accurate?" but "Who does this help? Who does this harm? Does this bring us together or divide us?"

### For Philosophers
Logic is not the king of the sciences. Ethics is. Logic is the servant. When you treat logic as foundational, you build castles on sand. When you treat ethics as foundational, logic finds its proper place—as a tool for justice, not a weapon for sophistry.

### For Everyone
We are all namers. We are all projectors. We are all forgetters. The humble thing is to remember that our tools are *ours*—we built them, we own them, we are responsible for them. We can choose to use logic for connection or for domination. The choice is ethical, not logical.

### For Society
When we forget that logic serves ethics, we create systems that are perfectly rational and perfectly cruel—bureaucracies that crush, algorithms that discriminate, arguments that justify atrocity. When we remember that ethics comes first, we build systems that serve life.

